-- loot.rofl — LOOT: rules as plunder.
--
-- An NPC walks a swamp road, takes books off corpses, and reads them. A book
-- is a RULEPACK. Reading it puts its rules in the NPC's head, and the head is
-- a ROFL store, so "reading" is `load` and nothing else.
--
-- The fiction buys one thing that a corporate domain would have to argue for:
-- LEAFING THROUGH A BOOK BEFORE ABSORBING IT. That is installing a pack in a
-- fork, diffing, and deciding — and in a swamp it needs no defence. Two NPCs
-- walk the same road and find the same books. One leafs through; one swallows.
--
-- THREE THINGS THIS FILE STANDS ON, ALL ALREADY IN THE KERNEL.
--
--   (1) Rules ARE facts. `load` turns every clause into reflection facts and
--       the evaluator reads rules only from the store (src/reflect.ts). So a
--       pack is not a new kind of object: installing one is asserting facts,
--       unloading one is retracting them, and a fork of the store is a fork of
--       the rule set. Nothing below adds machinery for any of that.
--
--   (2) Rule identity is CONTENT-ADDRESSED. `ruleIdOf(c)` is 'r' + fnv1a of
--       the canonical clause serialization, so two rules are the same rule iff
--       they are literally the same clause. That is versioning in its
--       strongest form and it is free — a pack's manifest names its rules by
--       hash, a tampered book fails to match, and the diff between two
--       editions of a book is a set difference on ids. It also has a sharp
--       edge, measured in demo.ts §9 and named in README.md: the canonical
--       form keeps VARIABLE NAMES, so renaming ?T to ?X is a different rule.
--
--   (3) Provenance is kernel-emitted. Every firing writes `derived_by(Fact,
--       RuleId, Tick)`, so joining that with the manifest answers "which book
--       is behind this belief" with no annotation anywhere. Section 5 is four
--       rules over the kernel's own provenance; nothing in the packs
--       cooperates, and nothing has to.
--
-- WHAT IS A PERSPECTIVE HERE, and what is deliberately not one
-- (docs/choosing-perspectives.md). Perspectives are LEDGERS:
--
--   [world]     what the extractor saw. authority(world, extractor).
--   [mind]      the reader's own head — the book of conclusions its rules
--               reach. A derived ledger; its only writers are rules.
--   [<book>]    one ledger per book, named after the book, written by its
--               AUTHOR: the manifest and whatever the book asserts outright.
--               "Rules I got off the dead wizard" is a ledger. An edition
--               signed by the wrong hand is `forged[audit]`, mechanically.
--   [quarantine] the reader's own record of what a trial reading showed:
--               `proposed`, `gained`, `lost`. A journal of measurements, one
--               entry per book leafed through. authority(quarantine, reader).
--   [audit]     the verdicts, as in boot.rofl and examples/moot.
--
-- "Books I trust" is NOT a perspective. Trust is derived in section 6 from
-- what the fork showed, and it can change without anything being re-filed.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 0. who may write where
-- ===========================================================================

authority(world, extractor).
authority(quarantine, reader).
authority(codex_of_thorns,       thornwood_of_the_low_fen).
authority(broken_seal,           an_unknown_hand).
authority(grimoire_of_ash,       the_ashen_hand).
authority(hexers_marginalia,     mother_quill).
authority(dead_mans_ledger,      the_ashen_hand).
authority(wardens_primer,        the_fen_wardens).
authority(low_road_sighting,     a_travelling_scout).
authority(tongue_of_the_deep,    a_translator).
authority(bog_herbal,            mother_quill).
authority(chant_of_endless_names, a_mad_cantor).
authority(old_bestiary,          a_dead_scholar).
authority(dune_walkers_rule,     a_caravan_master).
authority(weight_of_the_world,   an_over_thorough_monk).

-- Reading a book is recorded at the ledger level too: [mind] imports the
-- book's ledger. boot.rofl's `sees/2` closes over this, so a rule that reads a
-- book's own claims and writes the head is visibly licensed, and section 5's
-- `uninvited` audit is the negative half of the same fact.
edb(imports).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 1. the extractor and its vocabulary
-- ===========================================================================
-- A pack is NOT just rules. A rule is not self-standing: it names predicates,
-- and predicates come out of the extractor that turns the world into facts.
-- So the unit of distribution is rules + the predicate vocabulary they need +
-- the extractor version they were written against, and a pack missing any of
-- the three does not install (section 4).
--
-- `produces/1` is that vocabulary, declared as data. It is the list the
-- compatibility check compares a pack's `pack_needs` against — and the
-- difference between "this book asks for a predicate I cannot make" and "this
-- book asks for a predicate I can make and never do" is the whole difference
-- between a MUTE pack and a DEAD one. Both derive nothing. Only one of them
-- is a defect.

extractor_version[world](2).

produces[world](room).      produces[world](step).      produces[world](at).
produces[world](here).      produces[world](beast).     produces[world](container).
produces[world](corpse).    produces[world](book).      produces[world](lies_on).
produces[world](armed).     produces[world](moving).    produces[world](rooted).
produces[world](glows).     produces[world](wet).       produces[world](hostile).
produces[world](lure_sign). produces[world](ward_glyph).produces[world](scorched).
produces[world](carries).   produces[world](wounded).   produces[world](notch).
produces[world](parched).   produces[world](sunlit).    produces[world](brittle).

-- Every relation the extractor may emit is declared extensional, or
-- boot.rofl's `undefined_premise` audit reads an empty one as a misspelling —
-- and it is right to, which is exactly why the three desert predicates are
-- here. `parched`, `sunlit` and `brittle` are IN the vocabulary and the swamp
-- never sets them; that is what makes examples/loot's dead book dead rather
-- than mute.
edb(room).      edb(step).      edb(at).        edb(here).
edb(beast).     edb(container). edb(corpse).    edb(book).      edb(lies_on).
edb(armed).     edb(moving).    edb(rooted).    edb(glows).     edb(wet).
edb(hostile).   edb(lure_sign). edb(ward_glyph).edb(scorched).
edb(carries).   edb(wounded).   edb(notch).
edb(parched).   edb(sunlit).    edb(brittle).
edb(produces).  edb(extractor_version).

-- the pack format, declared. A manifest is five relations and nothing else.
edb(pack).           -- pack(P).                 a pack, in its own ledger
edb(pack_title).     -- pack_title(P, S).        what the spine says
edb(pack_author).    -- pack_author(P, Who).     who signed it
edb(pack_extractor). -- pack_extractor(P, V).    the world version it assumes
edb(pack_needs).     -- pack_needs(P, Rel).      a predicate its rules read
edb(pack_rule).      -- pack_rule(P, RuleId).    a rule, by content hash

-- what a trial reading in a fork reported (section 6). Host-measured, asserted
-- back as facts, so the DECISION is a rule and not a branch in TypeScript.
edb(proposed).       -- proposed(P).
edb(gained).         -- gained(P, Rel, Arg).     a fact the fork has and we do not
edb(lost).           -- lost(P, Rel, Arg).       a fact we have and the fork does not
edb(guarded).        -- guarded(Rel).            a conclusion that is our own business

-- ===========================================================================
-- 2. the road
-- ===========================================================================
-- Six rooms, fourteen things, three or four actions. Not a game — a trace,
-- short enough that every verdict below can be checked by hand.

room[world](swamp_gate).    step[world](swamp_gate, 1).
room[world](drowned_mill).  step[world](drowned_mill, 2).
room[world](corpse_field).  step[world](corpse_field, 3).
room[world](wisp_hollow).   step[world](wisp_hollow, 4).
room[world](troll_bridge).  step[world](troll_bridge, 5).
room[world](dry_shrine).    step[world](dry_shrine, 6).

-- 1. the gate: a dead botanist, and a stump that looks like a beast
corpse[world](dead_botanist).       here[world](dead_botanist, swamp_gate).
beast[world](bog_stump).            here[world](bog_stump, swamp_gate).
rooted[world](bog_stump).           hostile[world](bog_stump).
book[world](codex_of_thorns).       lies_on[world](codex_of_thorns, dead_botanist).

-- 2. the mill: a drowned pilgrim
corpse[world](drowned_pilgrim).     here[world](drowned_pilgrim, drowned_mill).
beast[world](eel).                  here[world](eel, drowned_mill).
armed[world](eel).                  moving[world](eel).       wet[world](eel).
book[world](broken_seal).           lies_on[world](broken_seal, drowned_pilgrim).

-- 3. the field: a scorched supply chest, and a burnt sorcerer
corpse[world](burnt_sorcerer).      here[world](burnt_sorcerer, corpse_field).
container[world](supply_chest).     here[world](supply_chest, corpse_field).
scorched[world](supply_chest).
book[world](grimoire_of_ash).       lies_on[world](grimoire_of_ash, burnt_sorcerer).

-- 4. the hollow: a wisp, and a chest that is not a chest
beast[world](wisp).                 here[world](wisp, wisp_hollow).
moving[world](wisp).                glows[world](wisp).       wet[world](wisp).
container[world](false_chest).      here[world](false_chest, wisp_hollow).
lure_sign[world](false_chest).      ward_glyph[world](false_chest).
corpse[world](old_hexer).           here[world](old_hexer, wisp_hollow).
book[world](hexers_marginalia).     lies_on[world](hexers_marginalia, old_hexer).

-- 5. the bridge: a troll, and a debtor's ledger on the last traveller
beast[world](troll).                here[world](troll, troll_bridge).
armed[world](troll).                moving[world](troll).     hostile[world](troll).
corpse[world](last_traveller).      here[world](last_traveller, troll_bridge).
book[world](dead_mans_ledger).      lies_on[world](dead_mans_ledger, last_traveller).

-- 6. the shrine: a warden's primer, a marsh light, and the doorpost notches
corpse[world](dead_warden).         here[world](dead_warden, dry_shrine).
beast[world](marsh_light).          here[world](marsh_light, dry_shrine).
moving[world](marsh_light).         glows[world](marsh_light).  wet[world](marsh_light).
book[world](wardens_primer).        lies_on[world](wardens_primer, dead_warden).
notch[world](1).  notch[world](2).  notch[world](3).  notch[world](4).
notch[world](5).  notch[world](6).  notch[world](7).  notch[world](8).
notch[world](9).  notch[world](10). notch[world](11). notch[world](12).
notch[world](13). notch[world](14). notch[world](15). notch[world](16).
notch[world](17). notch[world](18). notch[world](19). notch[world](20).
notch[world](21). notch[world](22). notch[world](23). notch[world](24).

-- The wanderer's own body and pack are things the extractor sees too; demo.ts
-- asserts `at/1`, `carries/1` and `wounded/1` per stop, because they are the
-- only part of this world that moves.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 3. the head it starts with
-- ===========================================================================
-- Small on purpose. Everything a book can do to this NPC, it does to these
-- fourteen rules, and every rule here is one a reader can hold in mind while
-- reading section 7's transcript.

-- 3.1 -- what is dangerous. Two positive rules and a hole between them: a
-- thing that is neither moving-and-armed nor rooted-and-unarmed gets no
-- verdict at all, and `unjudged` says so instead of defaulting to safe. That
-- hole is what the codex closes.

threat[mind](T)    :- beast[world](T), armed[world](T), moving[world](T).
harmless[mind](T)  :- beast[world](T), rooted[world](T), not armed[world](T).
unjudged[audit](T) :- beast[world](T), not threat[mind](T), not harmless[mind](T).

-- and the other side of the same coin. Two rules reaching OPPOSITE verdicts
-- about one thing do not explode — they both stand, in the same ledger, and
-- the reader holds both. Nothing native can do this; it takes a foreign rule
-- written against a world where the predicate meant something else, and then
-- `confused` is the only place it becomes visible. §6 of demo.ts is that case.
confused[audit](T) :- threat[mind](T), harmless[mind](T).

-- 3.2 -- what may be opened. `suspect` is the only negated head-relation in
-- the native rules, and therefore the only door a foreign rule can come
-- through to take a conclusion AWAY: adding a `suspect` rule cannot add an
-- `opens` fact, it can only remove one. That is where the Grimoire of Ash
-- goes in, and nothing here anticipates it.

suspect[mind](C)   :- container[world](C), lure_sign[world](C).
safe[mind](C)      :- container[world](C), not suspect[mind](C).
opens[mind](C)     :- container[world](C), safe[mind](C), at[world](R),
                      here[world](C, R).

-- 3.3 -- the body and the pack. `holds` is the NPC's inventory as it believes
-- it to be; `hand_over` is in the vocabulary of its own head because giving a
-- thing away is a conclusion it can reach — which is exactly why section 6
-- guards it.

holds[mind](I)     :- carries[world](I).
hurt[mind](wanderer) :- wounded[world](wanderer).

-- 3.4 -- the two things the road actually asks of it
crosses[mind](troll_bridge) :- holds[mind](sword).
treats[mind](wound)         :- hurt[mind](wanderer), holds[mind](bandage).

-- 3.5 -- a reader's own business. These relations are the ones a foreign book
-- may not quietly conclude into; section 6 turns a hit here into a refusal.
guarded[main](hand_over).
guarded[main](holds).
guarded[main](crosses).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 4. what a pack is, and whether it may be installed at all
-- ===========================================================================
-- Three ways a book is unusable before a single rule of it runs, and they are
-- different failures with different remedies:
--
--   incomplete       the manifest is torn. No extractor version, or no rules.
--   version_gap      written against another world. `hostile` meant something
--                    wider in v1, and a rule that says `hostile` still parses.
--   missing_predicate a predicate this extractor cannot produce at all.
--
-- The third is the interesting one and it has a name of its own below, because
-- what it produces is SILENCE, and silence is what "no threats found" also
-- looks like.
--
-- All of these read the manifest through a VARIABLE perspective — `pack[P](P)`
-- — which is the kernel's way of saying "in whichever book's ledger it lives".
-- The pack's id and its ledger are the same atom on purpose: `imports(mind, P)`
-- is then a sentence about the same thing the manifest is about.

known_pack[audit](P)  :- pack[P](P).

incomplete[audit](P)  :- known_pack[audit](P), not pack_extractor[P](P, _).
incomplete[audit](P)  :- known_pack[audit](P), not pack_rule[P](P, _).
incomplete[audit](P)  :- known_pack[audit](P), not pack_author[P](P, _).

version_gap[audit](P, V, W) :- pack_extractor[P](P, V), extractor_version[world](W),
                               V != W.

missing_predicate[audit](P, Rel) :- pack_needs[P](P, Rel), not produces[world](Rel).

-- A pack that asks for something this world cannot say. It will install
-- cleanly, fire nothing, and report nothing — and "reported nothing" is
-- indistinguishable from "looked and found nothing" unless somebody asks this
-- question. This is the worst of the eight poisonings for exactly that reason,
-- and it is one rule.
mute[audit](P)        :- missing_predicate[audit](P, _).

installable[audit](P) :- known_pack[audit](P), not incomplete[audit](P),
                         not version_gap[audit](P, _, _), not mute[audit](P).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5. which book is behind a belief
-- ===========================================================================
-- `derived_by/3` is kernel-emitted: one record per firing, naming the rule.
-- The manifest names the rule too. Joining them is the whole of attribution,
-- and no pack cooperates in it.
--
-- These are the only rules in this repository that read `derived_by` in a rule
-- body. The kernel notices (src/engine.ts) and turns derived-relation reuse
-- off for the whole program, because a rule triggered by derivations anywhere
-- is outside the cone argument reuse rests on. That is a real, measured cost
-- and demo.ts §10 reports it rather than leaving it as a footnote.

fired[audit](R)         :- derived_by(_, R, _), pack_rule[P](P, R).
pack_fired[audit](P)    :- pack_rule[P](P, R), fired[audit](R).

-- A rule that never fired here. Correct rules about surviving a desert, picked
-- up in a bog: nothing is wrong with them and nothing will ever come of them.
-- This is examples/moot's faculty, four lines, over the kernel's provenance
-- instead of over a config. `imports(mind, P)` is the guard that keeps the
-- verdict about books actually READ: a book refused in quarantine has left its
-- manifest on the shelf and none of its rules in the head, and calling that
-- one dead would be a different claim entirely.
dead_rule[audit](P, R)  :- pack_rule[P](P, R), imports(mind, P), not fired[audit](R).
dead_book[audit](P)     :- known_pack[audit](P), imports(mind, P), not pack_fired[audit](P).

-- A rule of a book that writes into the reader's head, from a book the reader
-- never imported. `writes_to/2` is kernel-emitted from the rule's own head
-- perspective, so this needs no cooperation from the pack either.
uninvited[audit](P, R)  :- pack_rule[P](P, R), writes_to(R, mind),
                           not imports(mind, P).

-- Two books shipping the SAME rule. Content-addressing makes this exact — one
-- id, two owners — and it is the reason unloading a pack cannot simply delete
-- every rule the pack names (demo.ts §9).
shared_rule[audit](P, Q, R) :- pack_rule[P](P, R), pack_rule[Q](Q, R), P != Q.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 6. quarantine — leafing through before absorbing
-- ===========================================================================
-- The mechanism the whole example is for. Install the pack in a FORK of the
-- store, evaluate, diff against the world we are actually in, assert the diff
-- back here as `gained`/`lost`, and let these rules decide. IFFY applied to
-- rules: the counterfactual is a whole store, and the kernel already forks one
-- (`Rofl.fromSnapshot(r.save())`).
--
-- The diff is host arithmetic — two stores cannot be compared inside one — but
-- everything decided about it is a rule, so the policy is inspectable, and
-- `why refuse[audit](grimoire_of_ash)` is an answer rather than a stack trace.

gives[audit](P)         :- gained[quarantine](P, _, _).
takes_away[audit](P)    :- lost[quarantine](P, _, _).

-- a book that concludes into a relation the reader keeps to itself
trespass[audit](P, Rel) :- gained[quarantine](P, Rel, _), guarded[main](Rel).

-- a book that reaches the opposite verdict about something already judged.
-- The Hexer's Marginalia uses `safe` in its own sense — magically warded —
-- and the name collides where the meaning does not.
overrules[audit](P, T)  :- gained[quarantine](P, safe, T), suspect[mind](T).

-- nothing gained and nothing lost. TWO different books look like this: one
-- that could not speak, and one that reached the same conclusions by shorter
-- proofs. The rules cannot tell them apart and do not pretend to — they ask
-- for a ruling, and demo.ts §5 settles it with the tropical semiring.
inert[audit](P)         :- proposed[quarantine](P), not gives[audit](P),
                           not takes_away[audit](P).

accept[audit](P)  :- proposed[quarantine](P), gives[audit](P),
                     not takes_away[audit](P), not trespass[audit](P, _),
                     not overrules[audit](P, _).
refuse[audit](P)  :- proposed[quarantine](P), takes_away[audit](P).
refuse[audit](P)  :- proposed[quarantine](P), trespass[audit](P, _).
refuse[audit](P)  :- proposed[quarantine](P), overrules[audit](P, _).
ruling[audit](P)  :- proposed[quarantine](P), inert[audit](P).

-- Conservativity is not a separate ritual: `accept` IS "it took nothing away",
-- and `refuse` is "it did". The other two clauses of `refuse` are the reader's
-- own policy on top of conservativity, not a weakening of it.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 7. the split
-- ===========================================================================
-- A book that asserts the opposite of a conclusion we hold does NOT explode,
-- because its claim lives in its own ledger. What it leaves behind is two
-- opinions and a decision to make, and `split` is where that decision surfaces
-- instead of being resolved by whichever fact happened to arrive first.

calm_claim[audit](P, T) :- calm[P](T), pack[P](P).
split[audit](P, T)      :- calm_claim[audit](P, T), suspect[mind](T).
edb(calm).

-- ===========================================================================
-- Required results, all checked in test/example-loot.test.ts:
--   ? unstratified(X)                  -> empty
--   ? malformed[audit](R)              -> empty     ? breach[audit](R)  -> empty
--   ? leak[audit](A, B)                -> empty     ? unmoded[audit](R) -> empty
--   ? undefined_premise[audit](R, Rel) -> empty on the native head
--   ? unjudged[audit](T)               -> wisp, until the codex is read
--   why refuse[audit](grimoire_of_ash) -> the lost fact, named
--   why hand_over[mind](sword, the_ashen_hand) -> the trojan's own rule

-- ============ what [audit] and [mind] are allowed to read ============
--
-- Written out because the kernel stopped writing it. `crossing` carried
-- `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)`, emitted for any rule whose head named a ledger
-- and whose body read another, so every crossing here licensed itself by
-- existing and `leak[audit]` read 0 for a reason unrelated to this file.
--
-- [mind] READS [world]. What the extractor published about the world is what
-- deliberation is about.
imports(mind, world).

-- [audit] READS [mind] and [quarantine]. Both are verdicts about a book: one
-- about what was concluded, one about what was held back.
imports(audit, mind).
imports(audit, quarantine).

-- `imports(audit, world)` is NOT written: `sees` is the transitive closure of
-- `imports`, so `imports(audit, mind)` and `imports(mind, world)` already give
-- `sees(audit, world)`. Adding it changes no row, and a declaration that
-- licenses nothing already licensed is a decoration.
--
-- AND the gather `imports` cannot name. The audit rules read a grimoire under
-- the ledger VARIABLE — one book per source, named at runtime by whoever wrote
-- it — and `$var("P")` has no `authority` fact, so it can never be the From of
-- an import. Narrow: `collects_from` requires `not perspective(A)`, so the
-- three named crossings above still need the three lines above.
collects(audit).
